Days after 9/11, India offered Washington full military cooperation and, within weeks of a car bomb at the Jammu and Kashmir Assembly, passed anti-terror laws that would later become the Prevention of Terrorism Act. That legal architecture, folded into the Unlawful Activities Act after POTA's repeal and widened under Modi in 2019, has since jailed rights defenders like Khurram Parvez for years without trial and sentenced Dukhtaran-e-Millat founder Aasiya Andrabi to life imprisonment over her speech alone, while India's security partnership with Israel deepened in parallel, from arms shipments through the Kargil war to India taking 34% of Israel's arms exports between 2020 and 2024.

That same post-9/11 vocabulary now travels globally: RSS chief Mohan Bhagwat toured London this month, protested by hundreds in Parliament Square, even as questions over whether his visit breached UK extremism rules went unanswered. The language born to describe attacks in New York and Kashmir together in 2001, the author argues, has since given cover to policies in Kashmir, from the 2019 autonomy revocation to mass detentions after the 2025 Pahalgam attack, that increasingly echo methods used against Palestinians.
